@100 = ~Unicorn Grove
Eaglehorn, guardian of Unicorn Grove, believes a mysterious magical shard is disturbing the creatures of the forest. He has asked us to seek out his friend Emhrys, a conjurer living in Beregost, and bring him to the grove.~
@101 = ~Unicorn Grove
Eaglehorn asked us to contact the conjurer Emhrys in Beregost about the strange shard that troubles the unicorns. We have not promised to do so, but Emhrys may be able to help if we decide to visit him.~
@102 = ~Unicorn Grove
The shard appears to contain memories capable of shaping our perceptions. Entering those memories may be necessary to reach and destroy the core of its magic. We have agreed to consider the danger before attempting the journey.~

@103 = ~Unicorn Grove
We have agreed to enter the shard and walk through memories of our lives. We must find the core of its magic and break its hold over the grove.~

@200 = ~The shard is still here, and its memories await us. Are you ready to enter?~
@201 = ~We're ready. Take us in.~
@202 = ~Not yet. We'll return when we're prepared.~
@203 = ~We have examined the shard together. When you are prepared to explore its memories, come and speak to us. We cannot enter until the passage has been prepared.~
@204 = ~Ah, there you are. I have been waiting for you.~
@205 = ~Have you spoken to Emhrys yet? He lives in Beregost, in the south-eastern part of town. Please find him. The unicorns are still distressed.~
@206 = ~I hope you have learned some manners since we last spoke. I am not inclined to engage in another contest of wits.~
@207 = ~I apologise for my behaviour last time. I went too far.~
@208 = ~I will not apologise because you cannot take a joke.~
@209 = ~Intruders, so far from civilisation?~
@210 = ~Pardon the intrusion, but we have a few questions.~
@211 = ~We were exploring when we found this grove. We did not mean to intrude.~
@212 = ~This place is boring. Nothing worth looting.~
@213 = ~My apologies. I was merely looking around. Goodbye.~
@214 = ~What sort of questions could you possibly have?~
@215 = ~Who are you?~
@216 = ~We were told you possess a magical shard that has been causing trouble.~
@217 = ~What is that artefact you are studying?~
@218 = ~That is all.~
@219 = ~That is all right. I am cautious because most visitors turn out to be bandits or monsters. Is there anything you need?~
@220 = ~Jests like that will hasten your banishment from this place. Raid a tomb or a cave if you must, but harbour no ill intent towards me or this grove, or you will suffer the consequences.~
@221 = ~Indeed you did. I trust it will not happen again.~
@222 = ~Then turn around and leave. You are no better than the common bandits who come here.~
@223 = ~Farewell. Take care as you pass through the grove, and do not anger the unicorns.~
@224 = ~My name is Eaglehorn, guardian of the unicorns. My task is to watch over this grove and protect it from all who would plunder it. No matter how deep within the elven woods we dwell, there will always be those bold enough to seek us out.~
@225 = ~Whether you are such people, or better ones, remains to be seen. For now, do not overstay your welcome in this sanctuary.~
@226 = ~I hope you are not a bounty hunter hungry for riches, because I will not hand it over. This shard came into my possession after bandits attacked the grove. Their leader carried it.~
@227 = ~Its purpose is unknown to me. Every attempt to identify it has failed. Whatever magic it contains surpasses my understanding. It is an extraordinary, perhaps ancient power.~
@228 = ~If ordinary identification magic fails, what else can we do?~
@229 = ~Edwin, could your knowledge be sufficient to analyse it?~
@230 = ~Jan, you look as though you have something to say.~
@231 = ~If even you cannot recognise it, I certainly will not be able to.~
@232 = ~I cannot say. What I do know is that the shard casts a strange, dark aura over the grove. The unicorns behave strangely, shrinking from even the lightest touch. Even the tasloi seem afraid to come near.~
@233 = ~Why would the creatures of the forest avoid this place at all costs? What is it they sense in the air? Danger, or something stranger still?~
@234 = ~Edwin, could your knowledge be sufficient to analyse it?~
@235 = ~Jan, you look as though you have something to say.~
@236 = ~What can I do to help?~
@237 = ~Could you not simply discard the shard?~
@238 = ~Throwing it away would not necessarily free the grove of its magic. It has already affected everything around us. We need to understand it before we act.~
@239 = ~I may not be able to uncover its secrets, but I know someone who might.~
@240 = ~Emhrys is an old friend of mine, a conjurer whose abilities far exceed my own. Sirian and I must guard the grove, so we cannot leave to seek him, but you could.~
@241 = ~He is a traveller who recently settled in Beregost. After years studying ancient scrolls, histories and forgotten lore, he is perhaps the finest scholar I know. If anyone can understand this shard, Emhrys can. Would you find him and ask him to come?~
@242 = ~His house stands in the south-eastern part of Beregost. Tell him Eaglehorn seeks his counsel for the safety of Unicorn Grove. He will understand.~
@243 = ~Then let us not tarry. We have a long journey ahead.~
@244 = ~I shall think about it. We have urgent business of our own.~
@245 = ~Here, take this gold for provisions should you need it. I shall await your return.~
@246 = ~I understand you have your own concerns, but please reconsider. Without your aid, I fear I cannot save the unicorns. You know where Emhrys lives if you change your mind.~
@247 = ~So its secret lies in memories. This is an ancient enchantment, and its strength is beyond anything I can identify. We must act before it affects the grove further.~
@248 = ~What can we do?~
@249 = ~Discarding the shard would not suffice, I fear.~
@250 = ~I fear that the only way to stop it is to extinguish its power from within, by entering the memories it contains. When you are ready, I will try to guide you there.~
@251 = ~How... how did you discover that after only a few seconds?~
@252 = ~Er... yes. Thank you for that captivating story, Jan. If the shard was truly invented by your family, can you reverse its effects upon this grove?~
@253 = ~Wonderful. I believe I can put you into a deep magical sleep and carry your minds into the shard. When you are ready, we can attempt it.~
@254 = ~Take what time you need. The shard and the grove are not going anywhere. Speak to me again when you are prepared.~
@255 = ~Emhrys and I have reached a fascinating conclusion. The shard stores the memories of whoever touches it and seems capable of bringing them to life.~
@256 = ~It seems that entering the shard may be the only way to silence the power within it. Are you willing to undertake such a risk?~
@257 = ~If that is all, I bid you farewell.~
@258 = ~Everyone is so uptight here. Do all elves forget what fun is?~
@259 = ~That was a rather poor joke, if you were joking at all... a-as I hope you were.~
@260 = ~Even the creatures we used to see in abundance are withdrawing.~
@261 = ~Tasloi are cowardly by nature. I would hesitate to blame the shard for that alone.~
@262 = ~They are wary, certainly, but they were never paralysed by fear. Tasloi are not the only creatures affected. Many animals that once visited the grove have vanished entirely.~
@263 = ~It is a difficult journey, but one I cannot make myself.~
@264 = ~It is a long way, but the unicorns are suffering. We should help him.~
@265 = ~Its power is bound to the grove. Remove it carelessly and you risk your precious unicorns. Our benevolent leader will no doubt volunteer us for yet another rescue. (What else is new?)~
@266 = ~Perhaps we could extinguish its magic from within the memories it contains.~
@267 = ~If we enter the shard, we may be able to destroy its core, though I cannot guarantee we would find our way back out.~
@268 = ~You see, it is quite simple, really. My Uncle Salebottom, bless his twitchy ears, married Aunt Espegonia during his infamous escapade in Neverwinter. Quite the scandal at the time! Mostly because Uncle Salebottom arrived in the city disguised as a rather large wheel of blue cheese to avoid some unpleasantness with the local tax collectors, but that is a tale for another day.~
@269 = ~Now, Aunt Espegonia? An enormously intelligent woman! Truly a marvel of gnomish ingenuity. She was wise, resourceful, and devised a brilliant little experiment that could store and release the memories of anyone who wished to share their past with loved ones. Or, in Uncle Salebottom's case, help him remember where he hid his spare trousers.~
@270 = ~She had but one tragic flaw, my dear Aunt Espegonia: she could not bear the smell of a turnip! Can you imagine? A culinary tragedy! But did that stop Uncle Salebottom? Not a chance. He chased her for three solid months, smelling faintly of turnip mash and desperation, until she finally agreed to marry him. Probably just to make him stop following her around.~
@271 = ~Can you get to the point?~
@272 = ~The shard is the creation of my very own flesh and blood! Though how it travelled all the way down here from Neverwinter surpasses even my knowledge. Come to think of it... Aunt Espegonia has not visited the family home in Athkatla for quite some time. Oh dear. Knowing her, she probably sold it to some outrageously wealthy, gaudily dressed merchant. Then some stinking, unwashed bandits must have ambushed the poor fellow, taken the shard, and brought it here. Yes! That is how it came here. It must be!~
@273 = ~How do we reverse its effects?~
@274 = ~Simple! We travel into the memories held inside, find the magic's source and shut it down. After that the shard should lose its power, and the forest can return to normal.~
@275 = ~The choice is yours.~
@276 = ~Am I the only one who objects to being imprisoned inside a memory? Just me? Ah, very well.~
@277 = ~We're ready. Take us in.~
@278 = ~We'll come back later.~
@279 = ~I am still studying the shard with Eaglehorn. There is no turning back once we enter its memories; we must be prepared.~
@280 = ~Guests. How lovely. Is there a reason you have barged into my home?~
@281 = ~Forgive the intrusion. Your friend Eaglehorn sent us. The unicorns are in danger, and he needs your help with a strange and powerful shard.~
@282 = ~Eaglehorn? The unicorns are in danger? That is grave news indeed. Thank you for telling me. I shall leave at once, and I take it you intend to help as well. Forgive me, but I cannot transport all of you such a distance. Long-range teleportation is... delicate. I trust you understand. I shall see you there.~
@283 = ~My apologies for making you travel all this way. It would have been dangerous to take everyone with me. I am glad you arrived safely.~
@284 = ~Not just any memories. The shard reaches for the experiences that shaped your choices, personality and destiny. It is immensely powerful. Its magic heightens fear, anger and joy in nearby creatures, which explains the unicorns' distress.~
@285 = ~Creatures that linger close to the shard for days or weeks may experience profound, inexplicable emotions. We ourselves have been close enough to feel little of its influence. But I believe I know how to contain it.~
@286 = ~I can place your minds in a magical sleep and carry your consciousness into the shard. You may encounter distorted, painful, even false memories there... particularly considering whose blood flows through your veins.~
@287 = ~You... know?~
@288 = ~I am an elven sage. Little escapes my notice, and I recognised you in Beregost. Your reputation precedes you, especially after what happened in Baldur's Gate.~
@289 = ~I have the spells necessary to transport your minds. You must find the source of the shard's magic and disable it. It could take the form of an object, a creature, even a memory. I cannot say. Stay alert and be prepared for anything.~
@290 = ~When the path to the shard is prepared, we can begin. Until then, do not touch it.~
@291 = ~I shall meet you at Eaglehorn's cabin.~
@292 = ~Bloody wizards! Not only do we have to trudge across the whole damn country, but now we've gotta trek all that way back? What a bleedin' joke!~
@293 = ~(Sigh) Let me have a look. Hmm. That's... peculiar. The magic stored within seems to reciprocate my touch, but... I can't get any farther than that. The shard operates as a ward and blocks magic users from ascertaining its origin and purpose. My findings are incomplete, and I doubt any of us can help it. Although...~
@294 = ~Although what?~
@295 = ~(Edwin falls silent, focusing all his energy on the shard. It begins to glow; Edwin suddenly drops to his knees and spasms in terror.) I saw... Thay! How is that possible? I saw myself as a child, and... my mother! What kind of trickery is this? I know of no magic so powerful!~
@296 = ~The shard lets its bearer revisit distant moments of their life, moments that shape their destiny. The Red Wizards of Thay would give anything for such an artefact. How did some filthy bandit get his hands on it?~
@297 = ~Myself? Hmm. Well, the shard feeds on memories and enables its bearer to see their own life's path. It penetrates the mind and uncovers the memories that shaped it.~
